<p>Joe Kent, the director of the US National Counterterrorism Center, resigned on March 17 in protest over the Trump administration’s war with Iran, becoming the first senior administration official publicly known to step down over the conflict. In a resignation letter he posted publicly, Kent said he could not support the war “in good conscience” and argued that Iran posed no imminent threat to the United States. His resignation quickly sharpened debate over how the administration justified joining military action against Iran. White House officials rejected Kent’s claims and said President Trump had compelling evidence that Iran was preparing to strike first, while PBS reported that administration officials had privately told congressional staff that US intelligence did not indicate Iran was preparing a preemptive attack on the United States. The episode matters beyond a single personnel change because Kent led the agency responsible for analyzing terrorist threats, and his departure exposes an unusually public split inside the national security establishment and the “America First” coalition over another Middle East war.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/0RLEczFx-wvS655_GyzxvtJF9xMmb0rwfiY3Cqt8Ekr7sVJPgwU4-oC6P6hzEUyrSJiTFy3uVKCatFomfngcWHpQg40I8Hvi71hSN5fGrN5k' alt='National Counterterrorism Centre Director Joseph Kent attends a House Homeland Security hearing on Capitol Hill in Washington, DC, on December 11, 2025.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Recent appointee: President Trump nominated Kent in February 2025 and the Senate confirmed him in July 2025, so he left less than a year after taking charge of the counterterrorism center.</li><li>War rationale: US and Israeli officials have described their strikes as preemptive moves meant to stop Iran from obtaining a nuclear weapon, while Iran has denied seeking one.</li><li>Political profile: PBS identified Kent as a former political candidate before he ran the National Counterterrorism Center.</li><li>DNI link: Semafor described Kent as a top aide to Director of National Intelligence Tulsi Gabbard, placing the dispute at the highest levels of the intelligence structure.</li></ul><blockquote>I cannot in good conscience support the ongoing war in Iran. Iran posed no imminent threat to our nation. - Joe Kent</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Joe Kent: Kent said the war did not serve US interests, urged President Trump to reverse course, and argued that pressure tied to Israel had pushed the administration into conflict. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cg4g66r3z40o'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>White House: Along with disputing Kent’s account, Semafor reported that one source close to the White House called his public resignation “a bit theatrical,” while another official said the White House had wanted Director of National Intelligence Tulsi Gabbard to fire him. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/03/17/2026/counterterrorism-officials-resignation-reflects-the-maga-rift-over-iran'>Semafor</a>)</li><li>House Speaker Mike Johnson: Johnson rejected Kent’s claim that Iran posed no imminent threat, reflecting support among senior Republicans for the administration’s view of the danger. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/watch-johnson-refutes-outgoing-counterterrorism-officials-claim-that-iran-posed-no-imminent-threat'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>Former NCTC leadership: Former National Counterterrorism Center director Nick Rasmussen said the resignation raises questions about how officials are weighing and communicating intelligence during the conflict. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/what-joe-kents-resignation-says-about-u-s-intelligence-and-counterterrorism-efforts'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/635334-us-counterterrorism-chief-resign-iran/'>US counterterrorism chief resigns in protest over Iran war</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://japantoday.com/category/world/top-counterterrorism-official-kent-resigns-over-trump%27s-iran-war-says-iran-posed-no-imminent-threat'>Top U.S. counterterrorism official Kent resigns over Trump&#x27;s Iran war, says Iran posed no imminent threat</a> - japantoday.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/us-counterterrorism-chief-joe-kent-resigns-over-iran-war/a-76402450'>US: Counterterrorism chief Joe Kent resigns over Iran war</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.boston.com/news/politics/2026/03/17/joe-kent/'>Top counterterrorism official resigns over Trump’s Iran war</a> - boston.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/video-top-us-security-official-resigns-over-trump-administrations-war-in/'>Top U.S. security official resigns over Trump administration&#x27;s war in Iran</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-03-17-0700/foreign-policy-significance-trump-appointee-joe-kent-resigning-over-iran-war'>The significance of Trump appointee Joe Kent resigning over the Iran war</a> - allsides.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cg4g66r3z40o'>Top US counterterrorism official resigns over Iran war, urging Trump to &#x27;reverse course&#x27;</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3346928/us-counterterrorism-chief-quits-says-israel-trapped-trump-joining-iran-war'>Top US counterterrorism official quits, says Trump was ‘deceived’ into Iran war</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/top-trump-counterterrorism-official-resigns-over-iran/'>Top Trump counterterrorism official resigns over Iran war</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/who-is-joe-kent-the-counterterrorism-official-who-resigned-over-the-iran-war'>Who is Joe Kent, the counterterrorism official who resigned over the Iran war?</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/03/17/2026/counterterrorism-officials-resignation-reflects-the-maga-rift-over-iran'>Counter